    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is anchored by **Corbin Carroll**, who commands the highest efficiency score of the day at 80.77. The matchup against Andrew Abbott is prime territory for a breakout; despite a minor platoon disadvantage, **Corbin Carroll** benefits from Abbott\u2019s 5.01 xERA and a generous park factor, setting him up as our primary engine for Runs and the scarcest category, Stolen Bases. Following close behind, **Gabriel Moreno** (66.77) and **Brice Turang** (62.16) capitalize on exploitable matchups against Abbott and Tyler Mahle, respectively. **Henry Bolte** rounds out our high-leverage starts with a 60.79 score; while he faces a difficult assignment against Cristian Javier, his slotting at the top of the order provides the volume necessary to remain in our daily rotation.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Seiya Suzuki**, whose 48.51 score kept him in contention until the final pass. While his base projection was solid, he was ultimately sidelined by a difficult matchup against Bryce Miller and an unfavorable park environment that suppressed his efficiency below the threshold of our active starters. We also analyzed **Jose Caballero**, who carries significant SB upside but is held back by a 13% platoon penalty and a 34% rain risk in his contest against Jos\u00e9 Soriano, making him too volatile to start. \n\nRisk management remains a primary focus, as all current starters remain subject to \"Lineup Pending\" volatility. We have mitigated exposure to elite arms like Shota Imanaga, where **Josh Naylor** faces a daunting 3.69 xERA; however, **Josh Naylor**\u2019s role in the heart of the order provides enough counting stat potential to retain his utility spot. We continue to monitor the weather closely, particularly given the reliance on **Mickey Moniak**\u2014who, despite a significant platoon drag, remains active due to his unique capacity for stolen bases against the slow delivery of Foster Griffin.",
